              {
                "type": "text",
                "text": "Express the silver’s unit mass in terms of the copper’s, then equate the combined mass to 150 g.",
                "marks": []
              }
            ]
          }
        ]
      },
      "worked": "Let $x$ grams be the weight of $1\\text{ cm}^3$ of copper. Then $1\\text{ cm}^3$ of silver weighs $\\frac{7x}{6}$ grams.\n\nThe combined weight is $150\\text{ g}$, so\n$$\\begin{align*}10\\left(\\frac{7x}{6}\\right)+5x&=150\\\\70x+30x&=900\\\\100x&=900\\\\x&=9.\\end{align*}$$\nThus $1\\text{ cm}^3$ of copper weighs $9\\text{ g}$.\n\nFor silver,\n$$\\begin{align*}\\frac{7x}{6}&=\\frac{7\\times9}{6}\\\\&=10.5.\\end{align*}$$\nThus $1\\text{ cm}^3$ of silver weighs $10.5\\text{ g}$.",
